sections.s2_technical:
THE PROBLEM — private field, leaky getter:

```java
public class Account {
    private final List<Transaction> transactions = new ArrayList<>();  // "private"

    public void addTransaction(Transaction txn) {
        validate(txn);  // validation: amount > 0, status = ACTIVE, etc.
        transactions.add(txn);
        auditLog("ADDED: " + txn);
    }

    // (Avoid) LEAKY GETTER: returns the LIVE internal list!
    public List<Transaction> getTransactions() {
        return transactions;  // returns REFERENCE to the SAME object!
    }
}

// CALLER BYPASSES ALL VALIDATION:
Account account = new Account("SAV2001");
account.getTransactions().add(new Transaction("FAKE", new BigDecimal("-999999")));
// Fake transaction with NEGATIVE amount added!
// No validate() called! No audit log! "Private" field MODIFIED from outside!
// Encapsulation = BROKEN.

account.getTransactions().clear();
// ALL transactions WIPED! No authorization, no audit! CATASTROPHIC.
```

THREE FIXES:

```java
// ═══ FIX 1: Collections.unmodifiableList — read-only VIEW ═══
public List<Transaction> getTransactions() {
    return Collections.unmodifiableList(transactions);
    // Returns a WRAPPER that throws UnsupportedOperationException on add/remove/set/clear
    // caller.getTransactions().add(fake) → UnsupportedOperationException!
    // BUT: if Transaction is MUTABLE, caller can still modify individual Transaction objects!
    // unmodifiableList prevents structural changes (add/remove), NOT element mutation.
}

// ═══ FIX 2: Defensive copy — new ArrayList ═══
public List<Transaction> getTransactions() {
    return new ArrayList<>(transactions);
    // Returns a COPY — caller modifies the copy, internal list UNAFFECTED
    // caller.getTransactions().add(fake) → adds to COPY only!
    // caller.getTransactions().clear() → clears COPY only!
    // Downside: creates a new list every call (memory/CPU cost for large lists)
}

// ═══ FIX 3: List.copyOf (Java 10) — unmodifiable COPY ═══
public List<Transaction> getTransactions() {
    return List.copyOf(transactions);
    // Creates an UNMODIFIABLE copy — best of both worlds!
    // add/remove → UnsupportedOperationException
    // Separate from internal list → even if we could modify it, internal is safe
    // Downside: no nulls allowed (throws NPE if list contains null)
}

// ═══ FIX 4: Stream — caller can only consume, not modify ═══
public Stream<Transaction> transactionStream() {
    return transactions.stream();
    // Caller gets a Stream — can filter, map, collect into THEIR OWN list
    // But CANNOT modify the internal list at all!
}
```

The MUTABLE ELEMENT problem — even unmodifiableList doesn't fully protect:

```java
// If Transaction is MUTABLE:
public class Transaction {
    private BigDecimal amount;
    public void setAmount(BigDecimal amount) { this.amount = amount; }
}

List<Transaction> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(account.getTransactions());
// view.add(fake) → UnsupportedOperationException (Recommended) (structural change blocked)
// BUT:
view.get(0).setAmount(new BigDecimal("-999"));  // MODIFIES THE ACTUAL TRANSACTION! (Avoid) 
// unmodifiableList blocks add/remove, NOT element mutation!

// FULL FIX: make Transaction IMMUTABLE (record, final fields, no setters):
public record Transaction(String txnId, BigDecimal amount, String status) {}
// Now: view.get(0).setAmount(...) → COMPILE ERROR! No setter!
// Immutable elements + unmodifiable list = TRUE encapsulation.
```

